questions.description:
`sumRange(a, b)` should sum every integer from `a` to `b`, **inclusive**. It doesn't — one of the endpoints is silently dropped.

```js
function sumRange(a, b) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = a; i < b; i++) {
    total += i;
  }
  return total;
}
```

Fix it in the editor, then click **Run** — it checks:

- `sumRange(1, 5)` should be `15`
- `sumRange(3, 3)` should be `3`

Once both pass, call your fixed `sumRange(10, 15)` and submit the result as `CTF{result}`.

questions.brokenCode:
function sumRange(a, b) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = a; i < b; i++) {
    total += i;
  }
  return total;
}

questions.correctCode:
function sumRange(a, b) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = a; i <= b; i++) {
    total += i;
  }
  return total;
}

questions.brokenCode:
function isSame(a, b) {
  return a === b;
}

questions.correctCode:
function isSame(a, b) {
  return a.length === b.length && a.every((v, i) => v === b[i]);
}

questions.description:
`reverseString(s)` should reverse a string with nothing else changed.

```js
function reverseString(s) {
  return s.split('').reverse().join(' ');
}
```

Run checks:

- `reverseString("abc")` should be `"cba"`
- `reverseString("hi")` should be `"ih"`

Once both pass, submit the result of your fixed `reverseString("flags")` as `CTF{result}` — lower case, exactly as returned.

`factorial(n)` should compute `n!` (`n × (n-1) × … × 1`, with `factorial(0)` defined as `1`).

```js
function factorial(n) {
  if (n === 0) {
    return 0;
  }
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
```

This one is subtle: it looks almost right, and it terminates, and it doesn't throw — it just multiplies every call by `0` somewhere along the way.

Run checks:

- `factorial(3)` should be `6`
- `factorial(0)` should be `1`
- `factorial(5)` should be `120`

Once all three pass, submit the result of your fixed `factorial(6)` as `CTF{result}`.

questions.brokenCode:
function factorial(n) {
  if (n === 0) {
    return 0;
  }
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

questions.correctCode:
function factorial(n) {
  if (n === 0) {
    return 1;
  }
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

questions.description:
`sumEvens(arr)` should sum only the even numbers in `arr`.

```js
function sumEvens(arr) {
  return arr.reduce((acc, x) => (x % 2 === 0 ? acc + x : acc), 1);
}
```

Run checks:

- `sumEvens([1, 2, 3, 4])` should be `6`
- `sumEvens([1, 3, 5])` should be `0`

Once both pass, submit the result of your fixed `sumEvens([10, 15, 20, 25, 30])` as `CTF{result}`.

questions.brokenCode:
function sumEvens(arr) {
  return arr.reduce((acc, x) => (x % 2 === 0 ? acc + x : acc), 1);
}

questions.correctCode:
function sumEvens(arr) {
  return arr.reduce((acc, x) => (x % 2 === 0 ? acc + x : acc), 0);
}

function sortAscending(nums) {
  return nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);
}

questions.description:
`makeCounters(n)` should return `[0, 1, 2, ..., n-1]` — built by creating `n` little functions that each remember their own index, then calling all of them.

```js
function makeCounters(n) {
  const fns = [];
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    fns.push(() => i);
  }
  return fns.map((fn) => fn());
}
```

Every function in `fns` ends up returning the SAME value — the loop variable's final value, not the value it had when that particular function was created.

Run checks:

- `makeCounters(3)` should be `[0, 1, 2]`
- `makeCounters(5)` should be `[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]`

Once both pass, submit your fixed `makeCounters(4)` joined by underscores as `CTF{a_b_c_d}`.

